{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8aa706b1dae1316e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tSpeech2Grasp: Data-Efficient Transfer of Text-Conditioned Grasp\n2\tDetection to Speech in Humanoid Robots\n3\t\n4\tarXiv:2607.26567v1 [cs.RO] 29 Jul 2026\n5\t\n6\tHung Nguyen1,∗ , Kim Nhat Minh Nguyen2,∗ , Van Duc Vu2 , Van-Danh Le3 , Hoang Huy Le2 ,\n7\tDinh Tuan Nguyen2 , Pham Tuyen Le2 , Van-Truong Nguyen2,4 , Quan Nguyen2,5\n8\tAbstract— Humanoid robots increasingly require multimodal understanding for natural interaction with humans. Despite the prominence of vision-language models, they generally\n9\tassume textual rather than the more natural speech inputs.\n10\tIn this paper, we investigate whether a well-established textconditioned model can be transferred to speech in a dataefficient manner. Using ALBEF as a case study, we conduct\n11\tdiagnostic analyses showing that a lightweight MLP-based projector effectively adapts it to speech, while preserving semantic\n12\tdiscrimination and robustness. Motivated by these findings,\n13\twe introduce Speech2Grasp, a framework for data-efficient\n14\ttransfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to speech. Realworld humanoid robot experiments show that Speech2Grasp\n15\toutperforms cascaded ASR-based pipeline, while reducing inference latency. Our findings suggest a practical paradigm for\n16\textending established text-conditioned systems to speech.\n17\t\n18\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n19\t\n20\tFig. 1. Methods for transferring text-conditioned models to speech. a) uses\n21\tan Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) model to transcribe the speech,\n22\tbut it mistranscribes, causing complete failure of the downstream task. b) is\n23\ttrained directly on speech, bypassing the discrete mistranscription problem.\n24\t\n25\tHumanoid robots increasingly require multi-modal understanding for natural interaction with humans [1]. To\n26\tsupport such capability, vision-language models (VLMs)\n27\t∗ Equal contribution. 1 University of California San Diego, 2 VinMotion,\n28\t3 Hanoi University of Science and Technology, 4 VinUniversity, 5 University\n29\t\n30\tof Southern California\n31\t\n32\thave emerged as the predominant foundation for languageconditioned perception. However, these models generally\n33\tassume textual language inputs [2], despite speech being a\n34\tmore natural and intuitive communication modality. While\n35\tfoundation image-speech models [3], [4], [5], [6] have\n36\tdemonstrated robust joint representations, adapting them to\n37\tdownstream robotic tasks may require collecting large-scale\n38\tspeech datasets together with corresponding task annotations,\n39\tfollowed by non-trivial architectural redesigns and computationally expensive fine-tuning.\n40\tA practical alternative is to endow existing image-text\n41\tmodels, which are already well established for the downstream task, with speech capability. The simplest solution\n42\tis to employ a pre-trained Automatic Speech Recognition\n43\t(ASR) model to convert speech into text for the textconditioned downstream model. However, such cascaded\n44\tpipelines are prone to higher latency and error propagation\n45\t[7]. As illustrated in Figure 1a, where grasp detection is\n46\tthe downstream task, if the ASR mistranscribes the prompt,\n47\tthe grasp attempt will fail entirely as the target object is\n48\tnot present. In contrast, models trained directly on speech\n49\tprompts may offer improved robustness to speaker and\n50\tacoustic variations [8]. However, as mentioned, fine-tuning\n51\tsuch models often incurs prohibitive dataset and computation\n52\trequirements.\n53\tThe above discussion motivates our work: transferring a\n54\twell-established text-conditioned robotic system to operate\n55\tdirectly on speech without using large speech datasets. In\n56\tthis paper, we choose ALBEF [9] as the pre-trained VLM\n57\tand language-driven grasp detection [10] as the downstream\n58\ttask, as a case study. Firstly, we investigate the representation gap introduced in the joint ALBEF embedding\n59\tspace when replacing text with speech. Our central finding\n60\tis that a lightweight MLP-based projector is sufficient to\n61\tadapt ALBEF to the new speech modality, without requiring large-scale data. Additionally, we perform diagnostic analyses showing that the adaptation retains semantic discrimination and robustness to unseen acoustics and\n62\tspeaker variations. Motivated by these findings, we introduce\n63\tSpeech2Grasp, a framework for data-efficient transfer of textconditioned grasp detection to speech. Our simulation and\n64\treal-world experiments on a humanoid robot demonstrate\n65\tthat Speech2Grasp achieves equal or better grasp detection\n66\trates than cascaded ASR-based pipeline, while incurring less\n67\tlatency due to bypassing the transcription step. Furthermore,\n68\tSpeech2Grasp only requires small-scale speech data for finetuning. More broadly, our findings suggest that lightweight\n69\t\n70\t\fmodality transfer can provide a practical paradigm for extending established text-conditioned systems to speech. In\n71\tsummary, our contributions are as follows:\n72\t• We conduct diagnostic analyses showing that ALBEF\n73\tcan be adapted to speech using robust, data-efficient\n74\tadaptation based on an MLP-based projector.\n75\t• We introduce Speech2Grasp, a framework for dataefficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection\n76\tto speech. Real-world humanoid experiments show\n77\tthat Speech2Grasp outperforms cascaded ASR-based\n78\tpipeline, while incurring less latency.\n79\tII. R ELATED W ORKS\n80\tCross-Modal Transfer to Speech. Generally, these works\n81\tdistill knowledge from large-scale pre-trained text models.\n82\tNi et al. [11] use an attention-based strategy to align textspeech representations for spoken language understanding,\n83\tusing only 10 hours of audio. Xie et al. [12] show that such\n84\talignment can be performed using a lightweight MLP-based\n85\tnetwork and an adaptive loss between the text and speech\n86\tembeddings, using only 4K speech samples. These works\n87\testablish that text and speech share common semantic representations, enabling data-efficient transfer between these\n88\tmodalities. However, such transfer has been relatively underexplored for robotic perception.\n89\tEfficient Adaptation for Grasp Detection. Generally,\n90\tthese works transfer representations from a large teacher\n91\tnetwork to a compact student. KufeNet [13] distills largescale knowledge into a lightweight model with unequal\n92\tRGB and depth contributions. LiteGrasp [14] uses featurelevel distillation with pseudo-label supervision from the\n93\tteacher model. PDCNet [15] integrates partial convolutions\n94\tand the wavelet transform to achieve feature-aware compact\n95\trepresentations. Although these works enable lightweight\n96\tdeployment without performance degradation, extensions to\n97\tmodality transfer remain largely under-explored.\n98\tLanguage-conditioned Grasp Detection. Vuong et al.\n99\t[10] introduce Grasp-Anything, a large-scale grasp detection dataset with natural-language text prompts. GraspAnything++ [16] extends with part-level annotations. GraspSAM [17] unifies prompt-based segmentation and grasp detection by adapting a pre-trained SAM [18]. Grasp-Anything6D [19] extends 2D datasets with annotated 3D point clouds.\n100\tHowever, these methods assume text as language input, leaving direct speech-guided grasp detection largely unexplored.\n101\tIII. D IAGNOSTIC A NALYSES OF ALBEF T EXT –S PEECH\n102\tR EPRESENTATIONS\n103\tA. The ALBEF module\n104\tALign BEfore Fuse (ALBEF) is a pre-trained visionlanguage model designed to learn semantically aligned\n105\timage-text representations [9]. As illustrated in the bottom\n106\ttwo blocks of Figure 2, the ALBEF fusion module combines\n107\timage and text features to produce the [CLS] embeddings\n108\tthat serve as their joint representation. Because the image and\n109\ttext modalities produce heterogeneous feature distributions,\n110\tALBEF employed an alignment objective during pre-training\n111\t\n112\tFig. 2. ALBEF [9] fusion between image, text and speech modalities. The\n113\tbottom two blocks show the frozen, teacher modules that perform imagetext fusion. Our diagnostic study analyzes the representation gap introduced\n114\twhen replacing text with the corresponding speech, as in the top two blocks.\n115\t\n116\tto project both modalities into a shared latent space before\n117\tfusion, hence its name. Motivated by the need to replace\n118\ttext with the more natural and intuitive speech modality for\n119\thuman-robot interaction, this Section investigates whether\n120\tspeech-conditioned representations can remain aligned with\n121\tthis shared latent space.\n122\tB. Text-Speech Representation Gap in the ALBEF Space\n123\tStarting from the pre-trained ALBEF, we replace the\n124\ttext modality with speech, while keeping the image input\n125\tunchanged. The image-text pairs were obtained from GraspAnything++ [16], a language-conditioned grasp detection\n126\tdataset that provides RGB images paired with textual grasp\n127\tinstructions. We use Kitten-TTS [20] to obtain the corresponding speech instructions. In total, we obtain 15K aligned\n128\timage-text-speech samples, with the train-validation-test split\n129\t80%-20%-20%. Afterwards, as illustrated in the top two\n130\tblocks of Figure 2, a pre-trained Whisper [21] encoder\n131\textracts the speech embeddings, which are subsequently\n132\tpassed through a trainable lightweight MLP-based projector.\n133\tThe projector maps the speech embeddings into the shared\n134\tALBEF latent space, and is trained through the following\n135\tobjective based on cosine similarity (CS):\n136\tLKD = 1 −\n137\t\n138\te⊤\n139\tS eT\n140\t∥eS ∥2 ∥eT ∥2\n141\t\n142\t(1)\n143\t\n144\twhere eS and eT denote the student (image-speech) and\n145\tteacher (image-text) [CLS] embeddings, respectively. The\n146\t“student” and “teacher” terminology reflects the fact that\n147\tthe image-speech branch is trained to reproduce the joint\n148\trepresentation generated by the frozen image-text branch.\n149\tTherefore, this objective is considered a knowledge distillation (KD) loss based on representation alignment [12].\n150\tFigure 3a shows the kernel density estimate (KDE) of the\n151\tCS between the student and teacher’s [CLS] embeddings.\n152\tThree variants of the student are evaluated: (i) without\n153\tthe projector using matched speech (gray curve), (ii) with\n154\tthe projector using matched speech (blue), and (iii) with\n155\tthe projector using randomly paired speech (orange). As\n156\t\n157\t\fFig. 3. Effects of the trained projector on text-speech representation gap.\n158\ta) The projector enables the image-speech student to closely align with\n159\tthe image-text teacher while retaining semantic discrimination. b) t-SNE\n160\tvisualizations show the projected student embeddings closely overlap the\n161\tteacher, while the unprojected embeddings remain separated.\n162\t\n163\tillustrated by the gray curve, directly using the speech\n164\tembeddings without the projector results in a substantial\n165\trepresentation gap. The average CS is 0.346 ± 0.088, indicating a weak alignment. This result is intuitive, because\n166\tthe ALBEF fusion module was pre-trained on large-scale\n167\timage-text pairs, rather than image-speech. However, the CS\n168\tremains positive, most likely because the image modality is\n169\tunchanged. The blue curve indicates that the trained projector\n170\tsuccessfully maps the speech embeddings into the teacher’s\n171\tlatent space. The average CS is 0.968 ± 0.027, indicating a\n172\tvery strong alignment using only a lightweight MLP-based\n173\tprojector. We hypothesize that this strong alignment arises\n174\tfrom three complementary factors: i) the shared image input\n175\tprovides a common visual context for both the teacher and\n176\tstudent, ii) the pre-trained speech and text encoders already\n177\tcapture similar high-level semantics, leaving the projector to\n178\tlearn primarily the modality-specific distribution mismatch,\n179\tand iii) alignment is evaluated using the fused [CLS]\n180\tembeddings, which summarize global information and are\n181\ttherefore easier to align than token-level representations.\n182\tInterestingly, comparing the blue and orange curves, where\n183\tthe latter corresponds to randomly paired speech, shows that\n184\tthe projector preserves semantic discrimination rather than\n185\t\n186\tFig. 4. Generalization of the trained projector to real-world conditions. a)\n187\tThe projector enables the student to closely align with the teacher, despite\n188\tlarge acoustics and speaker variations. b) t-SNE visualizations show both\n189\tcorrupted and clean student embeddings closely overlap the teacher.\n190\t\n191\tcollapsing different speech inputs to similar embeddings. The\n192\taverage CS of random pairs is 0.835 ± 0.069, which remains\n193\tconsiderably lower than that of matched pairs. Furthermore,\n194\tthe matched model yields a higher CS than the shuffled\n195\tmodel in 98.2% of the test instances. Before projection, the\n196\tformer only beats the latter in 50.7% of the test instances.\n197\tFinally, the broad distribution of the orange curve highlights\n198\tmuch greater variability while the blue curve remains highly\n199\tconcentrated, indicating that the projector does not produce\n200\tuniformly similar embeddings for mismatched instructions.\n201\tFigure 3b presents the t-SNE [22] visualizations of the\n202\t[CLS] embeddings for i) the teacher (green), ii) the unprojected (gray) and iii) the projected student (blue). The\n203\tprojected student embeddings closely overlap the teacher\n204\tembeddings, whereas the unprojected embeddings remain\n205\tseparated. These results indicate that the projector aligns\n206\tthe student embedding with the teacher structurally, complementing the high pairwise CS.\n207\tC. Generalization to Unseen Speakers and Acoustic Conditions\n208\tThe previous analyses demonstrate that the image-speech\n209\tand image-text [CLS] embeddings are sufficiently aligned\n210\t\n211\t\fthat a lightweight MLP-based projector can effectively bridge\n212\tthe remaining representation gap while retaining semantic\n213\tdiscrimination. However, real-world speech is often affected\n214\tby many factors, among which are i) acoustic conditions,\n215\tsuch as environmental noise and room reverberation, and ii)\n216\tspeaker variability. Therefore, in this Section, we investigate\n217\twhether the projector, which was trained exclusively on clean\n218\tspeeches of a single voice, generalizes to these conditions.\n219\tFirstly, from the 3K aligned image-text pairs in the test set,\n220\twe additionally synthesize 21K speech samples by rendering\n221\teach text instruction using the seven unseen voices provided\n222\tby Kitten-TTS. Afterwards, each sample is acoustically perturbed according to the following model:\n223\ty(t) = x(t) ∗ h(t) + αn(t),\n224\t\n225\t(2)\n226\t\n227\twhere x(t) denotes the clean speech, ∗ denotes convolution\n228\tand h(t) is the room impulse response (RIR). n(t) represents\n229\tthe environmental noise, and α is a scaling factor for a signalto-noise ratio of 8 dB, where the speech remains intelligible\n230\twhile noticeably degraded. We construct four conditions\n231\tusing combinations of RIRs and noise samples obtained from\n232\t[23]. In total, we obtain 84K unseen speech samples that vary\n233\tin both acoustics and speaker, together with the original 15K.\n234\tThe subplots in Figure 4a present the KDE curves\n235\tfor these conditions. Each KDE curve corresponds to a\n236\tspeaker, where the dotted black one denotes the training\n237\tvoice (“Jasper”). Overall, these results demonstrate graceful\n238\tdegradation: although real-world speech reduces the CS, the\n239\tprojector preserves a high degree of alignment, as the curves\n240\tonly move slightly to the left. For the worst-case scenario\n241\t(“Bella” voice, point-source interference), the average CS is\n242\t0.915 ± 0.070, which still indicates a good alignment. The\n243\tunderlying explanation is identical to the previous Section:\n244\tshared image inputs, shared semantic representations, and\n245\tthe ALBEF [CLS] embedding. Whisper’s large-scale pretraining on acoustically diverse speech may also contribute to\n246\tthis robustness. Figure 4b presents the t-SNE visualizations\n247\tfor i) the teacher (green), ii) the student (blue), and iii) the\n248\tstudent evaluated in the worst-case scenario (red). While the\n249\tworst-case embeddings exhibit moderate shifts, they remain\n250\tstructurally similar to both the teacher and the clean student\n251\tembeddings.\n252\tD. Implications of the Diagnostic Analyses\n253\tCollectively, the analyses indicate that a lightweight MLPbased projector can effectively align the ALBEF [CLS] embeddings of the image-text teacher and image-speech student,\n254\twhile preserving semantic discrimination and robustness\n255\tunder the evaluated acoustic perturbations. This suggests that\n256\tthe semantic knowledge of ALBEF-based, text-conditioned\n257\tmodels can be transferred to speech through lightweight\n258\tadaptation. Furthermore, successful generalization from 9K\n259\tpaired image-speech training samples to 84K unseen and\n260\tcorrupted speech samples suggests that such adaptation may\n261\tnot require prohibitively large speech datasets. Finally, such\n262\tsystems might operate directly from speech, without the\n263\tneed for transcription, while remaining robust to real-world\n264\t\n265\tconditions. These observations motivate the architectural\n266\tchoices and dataset design presented in the next Section for\n267\tdata-efficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to\n268\tspeech.\n269\tIV. M ETHODOLOGY\n270\tA. Problem Statement\n271\tBuilding upon the previous analyses, we formulate the\n272\tproblem of data-efficient transfer of ALBEF-based textconditioned grasp detection to speech. Let I denote an RGB\n273\timage, T a textual grasp instruction, and S its corresponding speech instruction. Given a pre-trained grasp detector\n274\tf (I, T ) → G, where G denotes the grasp map prediction,\n275\tour objective is to construct a speech-conditioned counterpart\n276\tg(I, S) → G that operates directly on speech. The adaptation\n277\tshould preserve the teacher’s grasp capability while requiring\n278\tonly a modest amount of paired image-speech data.\n279\tB. Overall Framework\n280\tFigure 5 presents an overview of the proposed\n281\tSpeech2Grasp framework. We first initialize the student\n282\tmodel g(I, S) from the pre-trained text-conditioned grasp\n283\tdetector f (I, T ), namely LGD [16]. As illustrated in Figure\n284\t5, Speech2Grasp consists of a visual branch (upper) and\n285\ta speech branch (lower). To inherit grasp capability from\n286\tthe teacher, in the visual branch, the grasp head is kept\n287\tfrozen, while the U-shaped encoder-decoder is only lightly\n288\tfine-tuned. To adapt to speech, the speech branch consists\n289\tof a pre-trained Whisper [21] encoder, as well as an MLPbased projector to map the speech embeddings into the\n290\tALBEF latent space. As demonstrated in the analyses, the\n291\thigh pairwise CS means the projected speech embeddings\n292\tbehave analogously to text embeddings. Therefore, similar\n293\tto the teacher, they can be injected into the same semantic\n294\tbottleneck for vision-language fusion, using the original\n295\tfusion strategy. Finally, the speech-aware, fused embeddings\n296\tare passed through the decoder and grasp head for final grasp\n297\tmap prediction. The total training loss is as follows:\n298\tL = Lgrasp + λKD LKD\n299\t\n300\t(3)\n301\t\n302\twhere Lgrasp is the task grasp loss and LKD is the knowledge\n303\tdistillation loss introduced in Equation (1). Lgrasp is detailed\n304\tas follows:\n305\tH\n306\t\n307\tLgrasp =\n308\t\n309\tW\n310\t\n311\t2\n312\t1 XX\n313\tgt\n314\tGpred\n315\tij − Gij\n316\tHW i=1 j=1\n317\t2\n318\t\n319\t(4)\n320\t\n321\tH and W denote the image height and width, and Gpred and\n322\tGgt represent the predicted and ground-truth grasp maps,\n323\trespectively. λKD is the balancing term. We employ an\n324\toff-ramp linear scheduler for λKD so that training initially\n325\temphasizes representation alignment, and gradually shifts to\n326\tfocus solely on the task loss. This strategy allows the speech\n327\tembeddings to rapidly adapt to the ALBEF latent space,\n328\twhile emphasizing the grasp supervision later.\n329\t\n330\t\fFig. 5. Overview of the Speech2Grasp framework for data-efficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to speech. An MLP-based projector\n331\tmaps speech embeddings into the ALBEF [9] latent space. A knowledge distillation loss, LKD , encourages the student’s projected [CLS] image-speech\n332\tembeddings to match the teacher’s image-text embeddings, enabling ALBEF’s reuse without large-scale paired speech annotations. The resulting [CLS]\n333\tembedding is injected into the visual semantic bottleneck to guide grasp prediction, supervised by the task loss Lgrasp .\n334\t\n335\tC. Speech Encoding and Projection\n336\tFigure 6 provides details about the speech encoding and\n337\tprojection. Firstly, the speech inputs are augmented according to Equation (2), although we sample all possible\n338\tRIRs and noise samples. The augmentation is performed\n339\ton the fly during training to simulate diverse acoustic\n340\tconditions without increasing dataset storage. Afterwards,\n341\tthe augmented speech signals are converted into log-mel\n342\tspectrograms [24], the expected inputs for Whisper [21].\n343\tWe then convert the Whisper outputs into a BERT-like [25]\n344\ttoken sequence, where the [SUMMARY] token is obtained\n345\tvia global mean pooling and captures the global speech\n346\trepresentation. The remaining [UTTER] tokens are obtained\n347\tvia adaptive pooling to preserve localized utterance-level\n348\tinformation. The trainable projector allows the pseudo-text\n349\tsequence to replace the actual one expected by ALBEF\n350\tfusion. The architecture of the projector is detailed in Figure\n351\t6, where the skip connection is used to reduce overfitting.\n352\tD. Frequency-aware Encoder and Decoder\n353\tTo maximize Speech2Grasp’s deployability, we improve\n354\tits robustness against camera noise often encountered in\n355\tpractical scenarios. We hypothesize that the encoder-decoder\n356\tin the visual branch should be frequency-aware. Precisely,\n357\tit should emphasize high-frequency (HF) components corresponding to object boundaries, which are essential for\n358\taccurate grasp localization. Furthermore, it should attenuate\n359\tHF noise arising from camera sensors.\n360\tFollowing previous works [26], [27], [28], [29], [30], we\n361\tuse the Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) to perform such\n362\tfrequency-aware decomposition. As illustrated in Figure 7,\n363\t\n364\tthe DWT decomposes an input image into four wavelet subbands (LL, LH, HL, HH), which capture coarse, horizontal,\n365\tvertical and diagonal information, respectively. Instead of\n366\tdirectly using the RGB image I ∈ RH×W ×3 , our framework\n367\tconsumes an input of dimension RH/2×W/2×9 , which is\n368\tobtained by concatenating the LL, LH, and HL subbands\n369\twhile discarding the noise-dominant HH. The explicit preservation of the LH and HL subbands retains the essential\n370\tobject boundaries, while discarding the HH suppresses noise\n371\twithout significantly sacrificing boundary information. Since\n372\tthe DWT performs downsampling, each subband is halfresolution. Therefore, although the input channel dimension\n373\tincreases, the computation overhead remains modest, following previous works [31]. While the encoder-decoder remains\n374\ttrainable, only its first few layers are optimized to adapt to\n375\tthe wavelet subbands, thus preserving grasp capability.\n376\tV. E XPERIMENTS\n377\tA. Simulation Experiments\n378\tWe evaluate Speech2Grasp on the Grasp-Anything++ [16]\n379\tdataset. The teacher model, LGD, is provided in the corresponding GitHub repository [16]. We use KittenTTS [20] to\n380\tsynthesize speech prompts according to the object frequency\n381\tin the LVIS [32] taxonomy. Among these objects, 70%\n382\tcorrespond to “Seen” (S) category and 30% to “Unseen”\n383\t(U) category. Our TTS generation produces approximately\n384\t15K speech prompts, totaling about 14 hours of audio. To\n385\tevaluate retention of grasp performance, we use the success\n386\trate across S and U categories, following [16]. We also use\n387\tthe harmonic mean (H) to summarize the overall success rate\n388\t[33]. In addition, we compare the inference latency required\n389\t\n390\t\fcessful transfer. However, because the transcription step is\n391\teliminated, the latency is reduced threefold. Finally, due to\n392\tthe shared semantic information, the adaptation only requires\n393\t15K samples. As indicated in Table III, the final CS is 0.94,\n394\tindicating that the projector effectively maps speech inputs\n395\tto text-like embeddings expected by the teacher.\n396\tMethod\n397\t\n398\tS(↑)\n399\t\n400\tU(↑)\n401\t\n402\tH(↑)\n403\t\n404\tLatency #S(↓)\n405\t(↓)\n406\t\n407\tLiteASR → LGD\n408\tSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n409\t\n410\t0.34\n411\t0.36\n412\t\n413\t0.33\n414\t0.33\n415\t\n416\t0.34\n417\t0.35\n418\t\n419\t102.2\n420\t36.6\n421\t\n422\t1M\n423\t15K\n424\t\n425\tLGD\n426\t\n427\t0.36\n428\t\n429\t0.33\n430\t\n431\t0.35\n432\t\n433\t–\n434\t\n435\t1M\n436\t\n437\tTABLE I\n438\tR ESULTS OF DATA - EFFICIENT T RANSFER OF G RASP D ETECTION\n439\t\n440\tFig. 6. Speech encoding and projection. The augmented speech is converted\n441\tinto log-mel spectrograms before being encoded by Whisper [4]. Global or\n442\tadaptive mean pooling is then applied to produce a token sequence similar\n443\tto the text-based BERT [8] representation.\n444\t\n445\tFig. 7. The DWT decomposes a noisy input image into four wavelet\n446\tsubbands (LL, LH, HL, HH). They capture coarse, horizontal, vertical and\n447\tdiagonal information, respectively. The LL is less noisy, the LH and HL\n448\tcapture object boundaries and the HH subband is dominated by noise.\n449\t\n450\tWe then measure robustness against simulated camera\n451\tnoise. We corrupt the input images in the test set using a\n452\tPoisson-Gaussian noise model [35]. We consider two noise\n453\tregimes. The “mild” setting uses λ = 30, σ = 0.01, while\n454\tthe “severe” setting uses λ = 10, σ = 0.02. Table II presents\n455\tthe results across 10 re-runs. Generally, without the DWT,\n456\tthe grasp detection performance degrades quickly, even when\n457\tthe speech prompts are clean. This occurs because of the\n458\tfused architecture, where noisy visual features can propagate\n459\tthrough the fusion module and degrade the final grasp predictions. Our DWT-integrated Speech2Grasp is more robust\n460\tbecause i) the LL is less noisy, and ii) the HH is discarded,\n461\talthough the model was not trained on noisy images. In the\n462\tcase of “mild” noise, our Speech2Grasp performs similar to\n463\tthe noiseless case in Table I, as the LH and HL preservation\n464\tretains the object boundaries crucial for grasp detection,\n465\twhile the overall geometry is still captured by the LL.\n466\tMode\n467\t\n468\tMethod\n469\t\n470\tS(↑)\n471\t\n472\tU(↑)\n473\t\n474\tH(↑)\n475\t\n476\tMild\n477\t\n478\tLiteASR → LGD (w/o DWT)\n479\tSpeech2Grasp (Ours - w/ DWT)\n480\tLGD (w/o DWT)\n481\t\n482\t0.31\n483\t0.34\n484\t0.32\n485\t\n486\t0.31\n487\t0.33\n488\t0.31\n489\t\n490\t0.31\n491\t0.34\n492\t0.32\n493\t\n494\tSevere\n495\t\n496\tLiteASR → LGD (w/o DWT)\n497\tSpeech2Grasp (Ours - w/ DWT)\n498\tLGD (w/o DWT)\n499\t\n500\t0.25\n501\t0.31\n502\t0.27\n503\t\n504\t0.24\n505\t0.27\n506\t0.24\n507\t\n508\t0.24\n509\t0.29\n510\t0.25\n511\t\n512\tTABLE II\n513\tR ESULTS UNDER SIMULATED CAMERA NOISE\n514\t\n515\tto produce the ALBEF [CLS] embedding, in miliseconds\n516\tand for the same speech. The number of training samples is\n517\talso recorded.\n518\tTable I presents the results, which were averaged across\n519\t10 re-runs. The first row corresponds to a cascaded pipeline\n520\twhere the clean speech is first transcribed using LiteASR\n521\t[34], an efficient ASR model that achieves performance\n522\tcompetitive with large models. This pipeline is the natural alternative for speech adaptation. The second row is our framework and the bottom row is the text-conditioned teacher. The\n523\tcascaded pipeline suffers from slight performance drops even\n524\tfor clean speech, due to the occasional mistranscriptions (examples provided in Table IV). Our Speech2Grasp achieves\n525\tsimilar performance to the text-only teacher, indicating suc-\n526\t\n527\tFinally, in Table III, we investigate the effects of the\n528\tnumber of speech samples on representation alignment, using\n529\t7.5K, 11K and 15K speech samples. Generally, increasing\n530\tthe number of speech samples improves representation alignment, and thus the grasp detection performance.\n531\tB. Real-world Robotic Experiments\n532\tOur real-world robotic experiments, which were conducted\n533\ton a Unitree humanoid robot [36], demonstrate the feasibility\n534\tof Speech2Grasp for practical deployment. Our speech commands were recorded in an uncontrolled office environment\n535\tusing a standard laptop microphone. We evaluated two scenarios: single-object and multi-object. In the latter, the robot\n536\t\n537\t\fMethod\n538\t\n539\tS(↑)\n540\t\n541\tU(↑)\n542\t\n543\tH(↑)\n544\t\n545\tCS(↑)\n546\t\n547\tSpeech2Grasp (7.5K)\n548\tSpeech2Grasp (11K)\n549\tSpeech2Grasp (15K)\n550\t\n551\t0.31\n552\t0.35\n553\t0.36\n554\t\n555\t0.29\n556\t0.30\n557\t0.33\n558\t\n559\t0.30\n560\t0.32\n561\t0.35\n562\t\n563\t0.83\n564\t0.87\n565\t0.94\n566\t\n567\tLGD [16]\n568\t\n569\t0.36\n570\t\n571\t0.33\n572\t\n573\t0.35\n574\t\n575\t–\n576\t\n577\tTABLE III\n578\tR ESULTS UNDER VARYING N UMBER OF S PEECH S AMPLES\n579\t\n580\tSpeech prompt\n581\t\n582\tASR mistranscription\n583\t\n584\t“Pick up spoon by its bowl shape”\n585\t\n586\t“Pick up spoon bites bowl\n587\tshaped”\n588\t“Grass plant at its leaves”\n589\t“Grasp sisters at its handles”\n590\t\n591\t“Grasp plant at its leaves”\n592\t“Grasp scissors at its handles”\n593\t“Grasp the bear then the bottle”\n594\t\n595\t“Grasp the pair then the bottle”\n596\t“Grasp the bear then the bottom”\n597\t\n598\tTABLE IV\n599\tE XAMPLES OF ASR MISTRANSCRIPTIONS OF CLEAN SPEECH ( TOP ) AND\n600\t\n601\tFig. 8.\n602\t\n603\tGrasp sequence of the humanoid robot using Speech2Grasp.\n604\t\n605\tREAL - WORLD SPEECH ( BOTTOM )\n606\t\n607\tVI. C ONCLUSION\n608\t\n609\tis instructed to grasp multiple unseen objects sequentially\n610\t(e.g., “grasp the bear then the bottle”). Specifically, the robot\n611\texecutes the grasp with the highest confidence, and inference\n612\tis then repeated until all objects have been grasped. None of\n613\tthe objects are seen during training or validation.\n614\tWe repeated the grasp attempts 30 times for each case,\n615\tand for two non-native English speakers. The total attempts\n616\twere 120. We present the results in Table V. The cascaded\n617\tpipeline requires a transcription step. However, because the\n618\treal-world speech commands are short and lack contextual\n619\tcues, they are more prone to mistranscriptions than clean\n620\tspeech. Table IV provides some examples. Once such errors\n621\toccur, the grasp attempt will fail completely because the\n622\ttarget object is not present, as illustrated in Figure 1a and\n623\tthe corresponding video submission. Our Speech2Grasp, natively trained on speech, provides a better grasp detection rate\n624\tdue to its tolerance to mild acoustic and speaker variations.\n625\tNote that the improvement in the simulation experiments\n626\tat Section V-A is modest because the speech prompts are\n627\tclean and synthesized, allowing ASR to perform well. The\n628\tlarger gains in the real-world experiments arise because\n629\tthe ASR produces more mistranscriptions. Speech2Grasp’s\n630\trobustness can be attributed to the speech projector, whose\n631\tresilience under much more challenging acoustic conditions\n632\twas demonstrated in the diagnostic study at Section III-C.\n633\t\n634\tPerson\n635\t\n636\tMethod\n637\t\n638\tSingle-object(↑)\n639\t\n640\tMulti-object(↑)\n641\t\n642\t#1\n643\t\n644\tLiteASR → LGD\n645\tSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n646\t\n647\t0.59 ± 0.03\n648\t0.70 ± 0.02\n649\t\n650\t0.54 ± 0.06\n651\t0.61 ± 0.05\n652\t\n653\t#2\n654\t\n655\tLiteASR → LGD\n656\tSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n657\t\n658\t0.61 ± 0.04\n659\t0.72 ± 0.03\n660\t\n661\t0.56 ± 0.07\n662\t0.63 ± 0.06\n663\t\n664\tTABLE V\n665\tR EAL - WORLD G RASP D ETECTION R ESULTS ON A H UMANOID ROBOT\n666\t\n667\tIn this paper, we first conduct diagnostic analyses to\n668\tshow that a data-efficient, lightweight MLP-based projector effectively aligns speech embeddings into the ALBEF\n669\tlatent space, while preserving semantic discrimination and\n670\trobustness to acoustic and speaker variations. Motivated by\n671\tthese findings, we propose Speech2Grasp, a data-efficient\n672\tframework that transfers text-conditioned grasp detection\n673\tto speech. Simulation experiments show that Speech2Grasp\n674\tachieves grasp performance comparable to the text-based\n675\tteacher, while being data-efficient and faster in inference\n676\tcompared to an ASR-based pipeline. Real-world experiments\n677\ton a humanoid robot demonstrate its deployability under\n678\treal-world acoustic conditions, while avoiding discrete mistranscription errors often occurring in real-world speech.\n679\tDiscussion and Future Works. Firstly, it would be\n680\tvaluable to investigate whether the proposed speech transfer\n681\tparadigm generalizes to vision-language architectures with\n682\tmulti-modal fusion strategies different from that of ALBEF.\n683\tSecondly, the teacher-student framework incurs substantial GPU memory overhead due to the forward passes of\n684\tboth teacher and student networks during training. Future\n685\twork will investigate cached knowledge distillation by precomputing teacher embeddings, enabling more memoryefficient speech transfer. Broadly speaking, we hope our findings suggest a practical paradigm for extending established\n686\ttext-conditioned systems to speech, supporting the growing\n687\tdemand for natural speech interaction in humanoid robots.\n688\tR EFERENCES\n689\t[1] J. A. Duncan, F. Alambeigi, and M. W. Pryor, “A survey of\n690\tmultimodal perception methods for human–robot interaction in social\n691\tenvironments,” J. Hum.-Robot Interact., vol. 13, no. 4, Oct. 2024.\n692\t[Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1145/3657030\n693\t[2] X. Yao, H. Zhou, O. Mees, Y. Meng, T. Xiao, Y. Bisk, J. Oh,\n694\tE. Johns, M. Shridhar, D. Shah, J. Thomason, K. Huang, J. Chai,\n695\tZ. Bing, and A. Knoll, “Bridging language and action: A survey of\n696\tlanguage-conditioned robot manipulation,” 2026. [Online]. Available:\n697\thttps://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10807\n698\t\n699\t\f[3] R. Girdhar, A. El-Nouby, Z. Liu, M. Singh, K. V. Alwala, A. Joulin,\n700\tand I. Misra, “Imagebind: One embedding space to bind them all,” in\n701\tCVPR, 2023.\n702\t[4] B. Zhu, B. Lin, M. Ning, Y. Yan, J. Cui, W. HongFa, Y. Pang,\n703\tW. Jiang, J. Zhang, Z. Li, C. W. Zhang, Z. Li, W. Liu, and L. Yuan,\n704\t“Languagebind: Extending video-language pretraining to n-modality\n705\tby language-based semantic alignment,” 2023.\n706\t[5] J. Han, K. Gong, Y. Zhang, J. Wang, K. Zhang, D. Lin, Y. Qiao, P. Gao,\n707\tand X. Yue, “Onellm: One framework to align all modalities with\n708\tlanguage,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer\n709\tVision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2024.\n710\t[6] Y. Wu*, K. Chen*, T. Zhang*, Y. Hui*, T. Berg-Kirkpatrick, and\n711\tS. Dubnov, “Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with\n712\tfeature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation,” in IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing,\n713\tICASSP, 2023.\n714\t[7] W. Tan, H. Inaguma, N. Dong, P. D. Tomasello, and X. Ma, “SSR:\n715\tAlignment-aware modality connector for speech language models,”\n716\tin Proceedings of the 22nd International Conference on Spoken\n717\tLanguage Translation (IWSLT 2025), E. Salesky, M. Federico, and\n718\tA. Anastasopoulos, Eds. Vienna, Austria (in-person and online):\n719\tAssociation for Computational Linguistics, Jul. 2025, pp. 56–75.\n720\t[Online]. Available: https://aclanthology.org/2025.iwslt-1.5/\n721\t[8] R. Prabhavalkar, T. Hori, T. N. Sainath, R. Schlüter, and S. Watanabe,\n722\t“End-to-end speech recognition: A survey,” IEEE/ACM Transactions\n723\ton Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 32, pp. 325–351,\n724\t2024.\n725\t[9] J. Li, R. R. Selvaraju, A. D. Gotmare, S. Joty, C. Xiong, and S. C.\n726\tHoi, “Align before fuse: vision and language representation learning\n727\twith momentum distillation,” in Proceedings of the 35th International\n728\tConference on Neural Information Processing Systems, ser. NIPS ’21.\n729\tRed Hook, NY, USA: Curran Associates Inc., 2021.\n730\t[10] A. D. Vuong, M. N. Vu, H. Le, B. Huang, H. T. T. Binh, T. Vo,\n731\tA. Kugi, and A. Nguyen, “Grasp-anything: Large-scale grasp dataset\n732\tfrom foundation models,” in 2024 IEEE International Conference on\n733\tRobotics and Automation (ICRA), 2024, pp. 14 030–14 037.\n734\t[11] J. Ni, Y. Ma, W. Wang, Q. Chen, D. Ng, H. Lei, T. H. Nguyen,\n735\tC. Zhang, B. Ma, and E. Cambria, “Adaptive knowledge distillation\n736\tbetween text and speech pre-trained models,” ICASSP 2023 2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and\n737\tSignal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–5, 2023. [Online]. Available:\n738\thttps://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:257106768\n739\t[12] Y. Xie, Y. Cui, L. Xie, and E. Yin, “Afd-slu: Adaptive feature\n740\tdistillation for spoken language understanding,” in ICASSP 2026 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n741\tProcessing (ICASSP), 2026, pp. 19 432–19 436.\n742\t[13] H. Nie, Z. Zhao, L. Chen, Z. Lu, Z. Li, and J. Yang, “Smaller and\n743\tfaster robotic grasp detection model via knowledge distillation and\n744\tunequal feature encoding,” IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters,\n745\tvol. 9, no. 8, pp. 7206–7213, 2024.\n746\t[14] L. Peng, R. Cai, J. Xiang, J. Zhu, W. Liu, W. Gao, and Y. Liu, “Litegrasp: A light robotic grasp detection via semi-supervised knowledge\n747\tdistillation,” IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters, vol. 9, no. 9, pp.\n748\t7995–8002, 2024.\n749\t[15] Y. Jiang, Y. Fang, and L. Deng, “Pdcnet: A lightweight\n750\tand efficient robotic grasp detection framework via partial\n751\tconvolution and knowledge distillation,” Computer Vision and Image\n752\tUnderstanding, vol. 259, p. 104441, 2025. [Online]. Available:\n753\thttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S107731422500164X\n754\t[16] A. D. Vuong, M. N. Vu, B. Huang, N. Nguyen, H. Le, T. Vo, and\n755\tA. Nguyen, “Language-driven grasp detection,” in 2024 IEEE/CVF\n756\tConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR),\n757\t2024, pp. 17 902–17 912.\n758\t[17] S. Noh, J. Kim, D. Nam, S. Back, R. Kang, and K. Lee, “Graspsam:\n759\tWhen segment anything model meets grasp detection,” in 2025 IEEE\n760\tInternational Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA), 2025,\n761\tpp. 14 023–14 029.\n762\t[18] A. Kirillov, E. Mintun, N. Ravi, H. Mao, C. Rolland, L. Gustafson,\n763\tT. Xiao, S. Whitehead, A. C. Berg, W.-Y. Lo, P. Dollár, and R. Girshick, “Segment anything,” arXiv:2304.02643, 2023.\n764\t[19] T. Nguyen, M. N. Vu, B. Huang, A. Vuong, Q. Vuong, N. Le, T. Vo,\n765\tand A. Nguyen, “Language-driven 6-dof grasp detection using negative\n766\tprompt guidance,” in ECCV, 2024.\n767\t[20] KittenML,\n768\t“Kittentts:\n769\tState-of-the-art\n770\tlightweight\n771\t\n772\ttext-to-speech\n773\tmodel,”\n774\tGitHub\n775\trepository,\n776\t2026,\n777\thttps://github.com/KittenML/KittenTTS.\n778\t[21] A. Radford, J. W. Kim, T. Xu, G. Brockman, C. McLeavey, and\n779\tI. Sutskever, “Robust speech recognition via large-scale weak supervision,” in International Conference on Machine Learning, 2022. [Online]. Available: https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:252923993\n780\t[22] L. van der Maaten and G. Hinton, “Visualizing data\n781\tusing t-SNE,” Journal of Machine Learning Research,\n782\tvol.\n783\t9,\n784\tpp.\n785\t2579–2605,\n786\t2008.\n787\t[Online].\n788\tAvailable:\n789\thttp://www.jmlr.org/papers/v9/vandermaaten08a.html\n790\t[23] T. Ko, V. Peddinti, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “A study on data\n791\taugmentation of reverberant speech for robust speech recognition,”\n792\tin IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n793\tProcessing (ICASSP), 2017.\n794\t[24] D. Jurafsky and J. H. Martin, Speech and Language Processing,\n795\t2nd ed. Prentice Hall, 2009.\n796\t[25] J. Devlin, M.-W. Chang, K. Lee, and K. Toutanova,\n797\t“Bert: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for\n798\tlanguage understanding,” in North American Chapter of the\n799\tAssociation for Computational Linguistics, 2019. [Online]. Available:\n800\thttps://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:52967399\n801\t[26] H. Nguyen, B. R. Li, and T. Nguyen, “Dwtnerf: Boosting few-shot\n802\tneural radiance fields via discrete wavelet transform,” 2025. [Online].\n803\tAvailable: https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12637\n804\t[27] H. Nguyen, R. Li, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Dwtgs: Rethinking\n805\tfrequency regularization for sparse-view 3d gaussian splatting,” in\n806\tProceedings of the 2025 IEEE International Conference on Visual\n807\tCommunications and Image Processing (VCIP). IEEE, 2025.\n808\t[28] H. Nguyen, A. Le, B. R. Li, and T. Nguyen, “From coarse to\n809\tfine: Learnable discrete wavelet transforms for efficient 3d gaussian\n810\tsplatting,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference\n811\ton Computer Vision (ICCV) Workshops, October 2025, pp. 3139–3148.\n812\t[29] H. Nguyen, R. Li, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Waveletgaussian: Waveletdomain diffusion for sparse-view 3d gaussian object reconstruction,” in\n813\tProceedings of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n814\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026.\n815\t[30] H. Nguyen, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Learnable multi-level discrete\n816\twavelet transforms for 3d gaussian splatting frequency modulation,”\n817\t2026. [Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14199\n818\t[31] H. Phung, Q. Dao, and A. Tran, “Wavelet diffusion models are fast\n819\tand scalable image generators,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\n820\tConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), June\n821\t2023, pp. 10 199–10 208.\n822\t[32] A. Gupta, P. Dollár, and R. Girshick, “Lvis: A dataset for large\n823\tvocabulary instance segmentation,” in 2019 IEEE/CVF Conference on\n824\tComputer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2019, pp. 5351–\n825\t5359.\n826\t[33] K. Zhou, J. Yang, C. C. Loy, and Z. Liu, “Conditional prompt\n827\tlearning for vision-language models,” in 2022 IEEE/CVF Conference\n828\ton Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2022, pp.\n829\t16 795–16 804.\n830\t[34] K. Kamahori, J. Kasai, N. Kojima, and B. Kasikci, “Liteasr: Efficient\n831\tautomatic speech recognition with low-rank approximation,” in Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural\n832\tLanguage Processing (EMNLP).\n833\tAssociation for Computational\n834\tLinguistics, 2025, pp. 2047–2061.\n835\t[35] N. Bähler, M. El Helou, É. Objois, K. Okumuş, and S. Süsstrunk,\n836\t“Pogain: Poisson-gaussian image noise modeling from paired samples,” IEEE Signal Processing Letters, vol. 29, pp. 2602–2606, 2022.\n837\t[36] Unitree\n838\tRobotics,\n839\t“Unitree\n840\trobotics\n841\tofficial\n842\twebsite,”\n843\thttps://www.unitree.com/, n.d., accessed: 2026-03-03.\n844\t\n845\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c127820d-1275-4f23-a408-2e5e13f5ae30", "uuid": "ec67c0fe-2e60-43e0-92b7-009ce490b50a", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T09:16:44.747Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "Speech2Grasp: Data-Efficient Transfer of Text-Conditioned Grasp\nDetection to Speech in Humanoid Robots\n\narXiv:2607.26567v1 [cs.RO] 29 Jul 2026\n\nHung Nguyen1,∗ , Kim Nhat Minh Nguyen2,∗ , Van Duc Vu2 , Van-Danh Le3 , Hoang Huy Le2 ,\nDinh Tuan Nguyen2 , Pham Tuyen Le2 , Van-Truong Nguyen2,4 , Quan Nguyen2,5\nAbstract— Humanoid robots increasingly require multimodal understanding for natural interaction with humans. Despite the prominence of vision-language models, they generally\nassume textual rather than the more natural speech inputs.\nIn this paper, we investigate whether a well-established textconditioned model can be transferred to speech in a dataefficient manner. Using ALBEF as a case study, we conduct\ndiagnostic analyses showing that a lightweight MLP-based projector effectively adapts it to speech, while preserving semantic\ndiscrimination and robustness. Motivated by these findings,\nwe introduce Speech2Grasp, a framework for data-efficient\ntransfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to speech. Realworld humanoid robot experiments show that Speech2Grasp\noutperforms cascaded ASR-based pipeline, while reducing inference latency. Our findings suggest a practical paradigm for\nextending established text-conditioned systems to speech.\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\n\nFig. 1. Methods for transferring text-conditioned models to speech. a) uses\nan Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) model to transcribe the speech,\nbut it mistranscribes, causing complete failure of the downstream task. b) is\ntrained directly on speech, bypassing the discrete mistranscription problem.\n\nHumanoid robots increasingly require multi-modal understanding for natural interaction with humans [1]. To\nsupport such capability, vision-language models (VLMs)\n∗ Equal contribution. 1 University of California San Diego, 2 VinMotion,\n3 Hanoi University of Science and Technology, 4 VinUniversity, 5 University\n\nof Southern California\n\nhave emerged as the predominant foundation for languageconditioned perception. However, these models generally\nassume textual language inputs [2], despite speech being a\nmore natural and intuitive communication modality. While\nfoundation image-speech models [3], [4], [5], [6] have\ndemonstrated robust joint representations, adapting them to\ndownstream robotic tasks may require collecting large-scale\nspeech datasets together with corresponding task annotations,\nfollowed by non-trivial architectural redesigns and computationally expensive fine-tuning.\nA practical alternative is to endow existing image-text\nmodels, which are already well established for the downstream task, with speech capability. The simplest solution\nis to employ a pre-trained Automatic Speech Recognition\n(ASR) model to convert speech into text for the textconditioned downstream model. However, such cascaded\npipelines are prone to higher latency and error propagation\n[7]. As illustrated in Figure 1a, where grasp detection is\nthe downstream task, if the ASR mistranscribes the prompt,\nthe grasp attempt will fail entirely as the target object is\nnot present. In contrast, models trained directly on speech\nprompts may offer improved robustness to speaker and\nacoustic variations [8]. However, as mentioned, fine-tuning\nsuch models often incurs prohibitive dataset and computation\nrequirements.\nThe above discussion motivates our work: transferring a\nwell-established text-conditioned robotic system to operate\ndirectly on speech without using large speech datasets. In\nthis paper, we choose ALBEF [9] as the pre-trained VLM\nand language-driven grasp detection [10] as the downstream\ntask, as a case study. Firstly, we investigate the representation gap introduced in the joint ALBEF embedding\nspace when replacing text with speech. Our central finding\nis that a lightweight MLP-based projector is sufficient to\nadapt ALBEF to the new speech modality, without requiring large-scale data. Additionally, we perform diagnostic analyses showing that the adaptation retains semantic discrimination and robustness to unseen acoustics and\nspeaker variations. Motivated by these findings, we introduce\nSpeech2Grasp, a framework for data-efficient transfer of textconditioned grasp detection to speech. Our simulation and\nreal-world experiments on a humanoid robot demonstrate\nthat Speech2Grasp achieves equal or better grasp detection\nrates than cascaded ASR-based pipeline, while incurring less\nlatency due to bypassing the transcription step. Furthermore,\nSpeech2Grasp only requires small-scale speech data for finetuning. More broadly, our findings suggest that lightweight\n\n\fmodality transfer can provide a practical paradigm for extending established text-conditioned systems to speech. In\nsummary, our contributions are as follows:\n• We conduct diagnostic analyses showing that ALBEF\ncan be adapted to speech using robust, data-efficient\nadaptation based on an MLP-based projector.\n• We introduce Speech2Grasp, a framework for dataefficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection\nto speech. Real-world humanoid experiments show\nthat Speech2Grasp outperforms cascaded ASR-based\npipeline, while incurring less latency.\nII. R ELATED W ORKS\nCross-Modal Transfer to Speech. Generally, these works\ndistill knowledge from large-scale pre-trained text models.\nNi et al. [11] use an attention-based strategy to align textspeech representations for spoken language understanding,\nusing only 10 hours of audio. Xie et al. [12] show that such\nalignment can be performed using a lightweight MLP-based\nnetwork and an adaptive loss between the text and speech\nembeddings, using only 4K speech samples. These works\nestablish that text and speech share common semantic representations, enabling data-efficient transfer between these\nmodalities. However, such transfer has been relatively underexplored for robotic perception.\nEfficient Adaptation for Grasp Detection. Generally,\nthese works transfer representations from a large teacher\nnetwork to a compact student. KufeNet [13] distills largescale knowledge into a lightweight model with unequal\nRGB and depth contributions. LiteGrasp [14] uses featurelevel distillation with pseudo-label supervision from the\nteacher model. PDCNet [15] integrates partial convolutions\nand the wavelet transform to achieve feature-aware compact\nrepresentations. Although these works enable lightweight\ndeployment without performance degradation, extensions to\nmodality transfer remain largely under-explored.\nLanguage-conditioned Grasp Detection. Vuong et al.\n[10] introduce Grasp-Anything, a large-scale grasp detection dataset with natural-language text prompts. GraspAnything++ [16] extends with part-level annotations. GraspSAM [17] unifies prompt-based segmentation and grasp detection by adapting a pre-trained SAM [18]. Grasp-Anything6D [19] extends 2D datasets with annotated 3D point clouds.\nHowever, these methods assume text as language input, leaving direct speech-guided grasp detection largely unexplored.\nIII. D IAGNOSTIC A NALYSES OF ALBEF T EXT –S PEECH\nR EPRESENTATIONS\nA. The ALBEF module\nALign BEfore Fuse (ALBEF) is a pre-trained visionlanguage model designed to learn semantically aligned\nimage-text representations [9]. As illustrated in the bottom\ntwo blocks of Figure 2, the ALBEF fusion module combines\nimage and text features to produce the [CLS] embeddings\nthat serve as their joint representation. Because the image and\ntext modalities produce heterogeneous feature distributions,\nALBEF employed an alignment objective during pre-training\n\nFig. 2. ALBEF [9] fusion between image, text and speech modalities. The\nbottom two blocks show the frozen, teacher modules that perform imagetext fusion. Our diagnostic study analyzes the representation gap introduced\nwhen replacing text with the corresponding speech, as in the top two blocks.\n\nto project both modalities into a shared latent space before\nfusion, hence its name. Motivated by the need to replace\ntext with the more natural and intuitive speech modality for\nhuman-robot interaction, this Section investigates whether\nspeech-conditioned representations can remain aligned with\nthis shared latent space.\nB. Text-Speech Representation Gap in the ALBEF Space\nStarting from the pre-trained ALBEF, we replace the\ntext modality with speech, while keeping the image input\nunchanged. The image-text pairs were obtained from GraspAnything++ [16], a language-conditioned grasp detection\ndataset that provides RGB images paired with textual grasp\ninstructions. We use Kitten-TTS [20] to obtain the corresponding speech instructions. In total, we obtain 15K aligned\nimage-text-speech samples, with the train-validation-test split\n80%-20%-20%. Afterwards, as illustrated in the top two\nblocks of Figure 2, a pre-trained Whisper [21] encoder\nextracts the speech embeddings, which are subsequently\npassed through a trainable lightweight MLP-based projector.\nThe projector maps the speech embeddings into the shared\nALBEF latent space, and is trained through the following\nobjective based on cosine similarity (CS):\nLKD = 1 −\n\ne⊤\nS eT\n∥eS ∥2 ∥eT ∥2\n\n(1)\n\nwhere eS and eT denote the student (image-speech) and\nteacher (image-text) [CLS] embeddings, respectively. The\n“student” and “teacher” terminology reflects the fact that\nthe image-speech branch is trained to reproduce the joint\nrepresentation generated by the frozen image-text branch.\nTherefore, this objective is considered a knowledge distillation (KD) loss based on representation alignment [12].\nFigure 3a shows the kernel density estimate (KDE) of the\nCS between the student and teacher’s [CLS] embeddings.\nThree variants of the student are evaluated: (i) without\nthe projector using matched speech (gray curve), (ii) with\nthe projector using matched speech (blue), and (iii) with\nthe projector using randomly paired speech (orange). As\n\n\fFig. 3. Effects of the trained projector on text-speech representation gap.\na) The projector enables the image-speech student to closely align with\nthe image-text teacher while retaining semantic discrimination. b) t-SNE\nvisualizations show the projected student embeddings closely overlap the\nteacher, while the unprojected embeddings remain separated.\n\nillustrated by the gray curve, directly using the speech\nembeddings without the projector results in a substantial\nrepresentation gap. The average CS is 0.346 ± 0.088, indicating a weak alignment. This result is intuitive, because\nthe ALBEF fusion module was pre-trained on large-scale\nimage-text pairs, rather than image-speech. However, the CS\nremains positive, most likely because the image modality is\nunchanged. The blue curve indicates that the trained projector\nsuccessfully maps the speech embeddings into the teacher’s\nlatent space. The average CS is 0.968 ± 0.027, indicating a\nvery strong alignment using only a lightweight MLP-based\nprojector. We hypothesize that this strong alignment arises\nfrom three complementary factors: i) the shared image input\nprovides a common visual context for both the teacher and\nstudent, ii) the pre-trained speech and text encoders already\ncapture similar high-level semantics, leaving the projector to\nlearn primarily the modality-specific distribution mismatch,\nand iii) alignment is evaluated using the fused [CLS]\nembeddings, which summarize global information and are\ntherefore easier to align than token-level representations.\nInterestingly, comparing the blue and orange curves, where\nthe latter corresponds to randomly paired speech, shows that\nthe projector preserves semantic discrimination rather than\n\nFig. 4. Generalization of the trained projector to real-world conditions. a)\nThe projector enables the student to closely align with the teacher, despite\nlarge acoustics and speaker variations. b) t-SNE visualizations show both\ncorrupted and clean student embeddings closely overlap the teacher.\n\ncollapsing different speech inputs to similar embeddings. The\naverage CS of random pairs is 0.835 ± 0.069, which remains\nconsiderably lower than that of matched pairs. Furthermore,\nthe matched model yields a higher CS than the shuffled\nmodel in 98.2% of the test instances. Before projection, the\nformer only beats the latter in 50.7% of the test instances.\nFinally, the broad distribution of the orange curve highlights\nmuch greater variability while the blue curve remains highly\nconcentrated, indicating that the projector does not produce\nuniformly similar embeddings for mismatched instructions.\nFigure 3b presents the t-SNE [22] visualizations of the\n[CLS] embeddings for i) the teacher (green), ii) the unprojected (gray) and iii) the projected student (blue). The\nprojected student embeddings closely overlap the teacher\nembeddings, whereas the unprojected embeddings remain\nseparated. These results indicate that the projector aligns\nthe student embedding with the teacher structurally, complementing the high pairwise CS.\nC. Generalization to Unseen Speakers and Acoustic Conditions\nThe previous analyses demonstrate that the image-speech\nand image-text [CLS] embeddings are sufficiently aligned\n\n\fthat a lightweight MLP-based projector can effectively bridge\nthe remaining representation gap while retaining semantic\ndiscrimination. However, real-world speech is often affected\nby many factors, among which are i) acoustic conditions,\nsuch as environmental noise and room reverberation, and ii)\nspeaker variability. Therefore, in this Section, we investigate\nwhether the projector, which was trained exclusively on clean\nspeeches of a single voice, generalizes to these conditions.\nFirstly, from the 3K aligned image-text pairs in the test set,\nwe additionally synthesize 21K speech samples by rendering\neach text instruction using the seven unseen voices provided\nby Kitten-TTS. Afterwards, each sample is acoustically perturbed according to the following model:\ny(t) = x(t) ∗ h(t) + αn(t),\n\n(2)\n\nwhere x(t) denotes the clean speech, ∗ denotes convolution\nand h(t) is the room impulse response (RIR). n(t) represents\nthe environmental noise, and α is a scaling factor for a signalto-noise ratio of 8 dB, where the speech remains intelligible\nwhile noticeably degraded. We construct four conditions\nusing combinations of RIRs and noise samples obtained from\n[23]. In total, we obtain 84K unseen speech samples that vary\nin both acoustics and speaker, together with the original 15K.\nThe subplots in Figure 4a present the KDE curves\nfor these conditions. Each KDE curve corresponds to a\nspeaker, where the dotted black one denotes the training\nvoice (“Jasper”). Overall, these results demonstrate graceful\ndegradation: although real-world speech reduces the CS, the\nprojector preserves a high degree of alignment, as the curves\nonly move slightly to the left. For the worst-case scenario\n(“Bella” voice, point-source interference), the average CS is\n0.915 ± 0.070, which still indicates a good alignment. The\nunderlying explanation is identical to the previous Section:\nshared image inputs, shared semantic representations, and\nthe ALBEF [CLS] embedding. Whisper’s large-scale pretraining on acoustically diverse speech may also contribute to\nthis robustness. Figure 4b presents the t-SNE visualizations\nfor i) the teacher (green), ii) the student (blue), and iii) the\nstudent evaluated in the worst-case scenario (red). While the\nworst-case embeddings exhibit moderate shifts, they remain\nstructurally similar to both the teacher and the clean student\nembeddings.\nD. Implications of the Diagnostic Analyses\nCollectively, the analyses indicate that a lightweight MLPbased projector can effectively align the ALBEF [CLS] embeddings of the image-text teacher and image-speech student,\nwhile preserving semantic discrimination and robustness\nunder the evaluated acoustic perturbations. This suggests that\nthe semantic knowledge of ALBEF-based, text-conditioned\nmodels can be transferred to speech through lightweight\nadaptation. Furthermore, successful generalization from 9K\npaired image-speech training samples to 84K unseen and\ncorrupted speech samples suggests that such adaptation may\nnot require prohibitively large speech datasets. Finally, such\nsystems might operate directly from speech, without the\nneed for transcription, while remaining robust to real-world\n\nconditions. These observations motivate the architectural\nchoices and dataset design presented in the next Section for\ndata-efficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to\nspeech.\nIV. M ETHODOLOGY\nA. Problem Statement\nBuilding upon the previous analyses, we formulate the\nproblem of data-efficient transfer of ALBEF-based textconditioned grasp detection to speech. Let I denote an RGB\nimage, T a textual grasp instruction, and S its corresponding speech instruction. Given a pre-trained grasp detector\nf (I, T ) → G, where G denotes the grasp map prediction,\nour objective is to construct a speech-conditioned counterpart\ng(I, S) → G that operates directly on speech. The adaptation\nshould preserve the teacher’s grasp capability while requiring\nonly a modest amount of paired image-speech data.\nB. Overall Framework\nFigure 5 presents an overview of the proposed\nSpeech2Grasp framework. We first initialize the student\nmodel g(I, S) from the pre-trained text-conditioned grasp\ndetector f (I, T ), namely LGD [16]. As illustrated in Figure\n5, Speech2Grasp consists of a visual branch (upper) and\na speech branch (lower). To inherit grasp capability from\nthe teacher, in the visual branch, the grasp head is kept\nfrozen, while the U-shaped encoder-decoder is only lightly\nfine-tuned. To adapt to speech, the speech branch consists\nof a pre-trained Whisper [21] encoder, as well as an MLPbased projector to map the speech embeddings into the\nALBEF latent space. As demonstrated in the analyses, the\nhigh pairwise CS means the projected speech embeddings\nbehave analogously to text embeddings. Therefore, similar\nto the teacher, they can be injected into the same semantic\nbottleneck for vision-language fusion, using the original\nfusion strategy. Finally, the speech-aware, fused embeddings\nare passed through the decoder and grasp head for final grasp\nmap prediction. The total training loss is as follows:\nL = Lgrasp + λKD LKD\n\n(3)\n\nwhere Lgrasp is the task grasp loss and LKD is the knowledge\ndistillation loss introduced in Equation (1). Lgrasp is detailed\nas follows:\nH\n\nLgrasp =\n\nW\n\n2\n1 XX\ngt\nGpred\nij − Gij\nHW i=1 j=1\n2\n\n(4)\n\nH and W denote the image height and width, and Gpred and\nGgt represent the predicted and ground-truth grasp maps,\nrespectively. λKD is the balancing term. We employ an\noff-ramp linear scheduler for λKD so that training initially\nemphasizes representation alignment, and gradually shifts to\nfocus solely on the task loss. This strategy allows the speech\nembeddings to rapidly adapt to the ALBEF latent space,\nwhile emphasizing the grasp supervision later.\n\n\fFig. 5. Overview of the Speech2Grasp framework for data-efficient transfer of text-conditioned grasp detection to speech. An MLP-based projector\nmaps speech embeddings into the ALBEF [9] latent space. A knowledge distillation loss, LKD , encourages the student’s projected [CLS] image-speech\nembeddings to match the teacher’s image-text embeddings, enabling ALBEF’s reuse without large-scale paired speech annotations. The resulting [CLS]\nembedding is injected into the visual semantic bottleneck to guide grasp prediction, supervised by the task loss Lgrasp .\n\nC. Speech Encoding and Projection\nFigure 6 provides details about the speech encoding and\nprojection. Firstly, the speech inputs are augmented according to Equation (2), although we sample all possible\nRIRs and noise samples. The augmentation is performed\non the fly during training to simulate diverse acoustic\nconditions without increasing dataset storage. Afterwards,\nthe augmented speech signals are converted into log-mel\nspectrograms [24], the expected inputs for Whisper [21].\nWe then convert the Whisper outputs into a BERT-like [25]\ntoken sequence, where the [SUMMARY] token is obtained\nvia global mean pooling and captures the global speech\nrepresentation. The remaining [UTTER] tokens are obtained\nvia adaptive pooling to preserve localized utterance-level\ninformation. The trainable projector allows the pseudo-text\nsequence to replace the actual one expected by ALBEF\nfusion. The architecture of the projector is detailed in Figure\n6, where the skip connection is used to reduce overfitting.\nD. Frequency-aware Encoder and Decoder\nTo maximize Speech2Grasp’s deployability, we improve\nits robustness against camera noise often encountered in\npractical scenarios. We hypothesize that the encoder-decoder\nin the visual branch should be frequency-aware. Precisely,\nit should emphasize high-frequency (HF) components corresponding to object boundaries, which are essential for\naccurate grasp localization. Furthermore, it should attenuate\nHF noise arising from camera sensors.\nFollowing previous works [26], [27], [28], [29], [30], we\nuse the Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) to perform such\nfrequency-aware decomposition. As illustrated in Figure 7,\n\nthe DWT decomposes an input image into four wavelet subbands (LL, LH, HL, HH), which capture coarse, horizontal,\nvertical and diagonal information, respectively. Instead of\ndirectly using the RGB image I ∈ RH×W ×3 , our framework\nconsumes an input of dimension RH/2×W/2×9 , which is\nobtained by concatenating the LL, LH, and HL subbands\nwhile discarding the noise-dominant HH. The explicit preservation of the LH and HL subbands retains the essential\nobject boundaries, while discarding the HH suppresses noise\nwithout significantly sacrificing boundary information. Since\nthe DWT performs downsampling, each subband is halfresolution. Therefore, although the input channel dimension\nincreases, the computation overhead remains modest, following previous works [31]. While the encoder-decoder remains\ntrainable, only its first few layers are optimized to adapt to\nthe wavelet subbands, thus preserving grasp capability.\nV. E XPERIMENTS\nA. Simulation Experiments\nWe evaluate Speech2Grasp on the Grasp-Anything++ [16]\ndataset. The teacher model, LGD, is provided in the corresponding GitHub repository [16]. We use KittenTTS [20] to\nsynthesize speech prompts according to the object frequency\nin the LVIS [32] taxonomy. Among these objects, 70%\ncorrespond to “Seen” (S) category and 30% to “Unseen”\n(U) category. Our TTS generation produces approximately\n15K speech prompts, totaling about 14 hours of audio. To\nevaluate retention of grasp performance, we use the success\nrate across S and U categories, following [16]. We also use\nthe harmonic mean (H) to summarize the overall success rate\n[33]. In addition, we compare the inference latency required\n\n\fcessful transfer. However, because the transcription step is\neliminated, the latency is reduced threefold. Finally, due to\nthe shared semantic information, the adaptation only requires\n15K samples. As indicated in Table III, the final CS is 0.94,\nindicating that the projector effectively maps speech inputs\nto text-like embeddings expected by the teacher.\nMethod\n\nS(↑)\n\nU(↑)\n\nH(↑)\n\nLatency #S(↓)\n(↓)\n\nLiteASR → LGD\nSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n\n0.34\n0.36\n\n0.33\n0.33\n\n0.34\n0.35\n\n102.2\n36.6\n\n1M\n15K\n\nLGD\n\n0.36\n\n0.33\n\n0.35\n\n–\n\n1M\n\nTABLE I\nR ESULTS OF DATA - EFFICIENT T RANSFER OF G RASP D ETECTION\n\nFig. 6. Speech encoding and projection. The augmented speech is converted\ninto log-mel spectrograms before being encoded by Whisper [4]. Global or\nadaptive mean pooling is then applied to produce a token sequence similar\nto the text-based BERT [8] representation.\n\nFig. 7. The DWT decomposes a noisy input image into four wavelet\nsubbands (LL, LH, HL, HH). They capture coarse, horizontal, vertical and\ndiagonal information, respectively. The LL is less noisy, the LH and HL\ncapture object boundaries and the HH subband is dominated by noise.\n\nWe then measure robustness against simulated camera\nnoise. We corrupt the input images in the test set using a\nPoisson-Gaussian noise model [35]. We consider two noise\nregimes. The “mild” setting uses λ = 30, σ = 0.01, while\nthe “severe” setting uses λ = 10, σ = 0.02. Table II presents\nthe results across 10 re-runs. Generally, without the DWT,\nthe grasp detection performance degrades quickly, even when\nthe speech prompts are clean. This occurs because of the\nfused architecture, where noisy visual features can propagate\nthrough the fusion module and degrade the final grasp predictions. Our DWT-integrated Speech2Grasp is more robust\nbecause i) the LL is less noisy, and ii) the HH is discarded,\nalthough the model was not trained on noisy images. In the\ncase of “mild” noise, our Speech2Grasp performs similar to\nthe noiseless case in Table I, as the LH and HL preservation\nretains the object boundaries crucial for grasp detection,\nwhile the overall geometry is still captured by the LL.\nMode\n\nMethod\n\nS(↑)\n\nU(↑)\n\nH(↑)\n\nMild\n\nLiteASR → LGD (w/o DWT)\nSpeech2Grasp (Ours - w/ DWT)\nLGD (w/o DWT)\n\n0.31\n0.34\n0.32\n\n0.31\n0.33\n0.31\n\n0.31\n0.34\n0.32\n\nSevere\n\nLiteASR → LGD (w/o DWT)\nSpeech2Grasp (Ours - w/ DWT)\nLGD (w/o DWT)\n\n0.25\n0.31\n0.27\n\n0.24\n0.27\n0.24\n\n0.24\n0.29\n0.25\n\nTABLE II\nR ESULTS UNDER SIMULATED CAMERA NOISE\n\nto produce the ALBEF [CLS] embedding, in miliseconds\nand for the same speech. The number of training samples is\nalso recorded.\nTable I presents the results, which were averaged across\n10 re-runs. The first row corresponds to a cascaded pipeline\nwhere the clean speech is first transcribed using LiteASR\n[34], an efficient ASR model that achieves performance\ncompetitive with large models. This pipeline is the natural alternative for speech adaptation. The second row is our framework and the bottom row is the text-conditioned teacher. The\ncascaded pipeline suffers from slight performance drops even\nfor clean speech, due to the occasional mistranscriptions (examples provided in Table IV). Our Speech2Grasp achieves\nsimilar performance to the text-only teacher, indicating suc-\n\nFinally, in Table III, we investigate the effects of the\nnumber of speech samples on representation alignment, using\n7.5K, 11K and 15K speech samples. Generally, increasing\nthe number of speech samples improves representation alignment, and thus the grasp detection performance.\nB. Real-world Robotic Experiments\nOur real-world robotic experiments, which were conducted\non a Unitree humanoid robot [36], demonstrate the feasibility\nof Speech2Grasp for practical deployment. Our speech commands were recorded in an uncontrolled office environment\nusing a standard laptop microphone. We evaluated two scenarios: single-object and multi-object. In the latter, the robot\n\n\fMethod\n\nS(↑)\n\nU(↑)\n\nH(↑)\n\nCS(↑)\n\nSpeech2Grasp (7.5K)\nSpeech2Grasp (11K)\nSpeech2Grasp (15K)\n\n0.31\n0.35\n0.36\n\n0.29\n0.30\n0.33\n\n0.30\n0.32\n0.35\n\n0.83\n0.87\n0.94\n\nLGD [16]\n\n0.36\n\n0.33\n\n0.35\n\n–\n\nTABLE III\nR ESULTS UNDER VARYING N UMBER OF S PEECH S AMPLES\n\nSpeech prompt\n\nASR mistranscription\n\n“Pick up spoon by its bowl shape”\n\n“Pick up spoon bites bowl\nshaped”\n“Grass plant at its leaves”\n“Grasp sisters at its handles”\n\n“Grasp plant at its leaves”\n“Grasp scissors at its handles”\n“Grasp the bear then the bottle”\n\n“Grasp the pair then the bottle”\n“Grasp the bear then the bottom”\n\nTABLE IV\nE XAMPLES OF ASR MISTRANSCRIPTIONS OF CLEAN SPEECH ( TOP ) AND\n\nFig. 8.\n\nGrasp sequence of the humanoid robot using Speech2Grasp.\n\nREAL - WORLD SPEECH ( BOTTOM )\n\nVI. C ONCLUSION\n\nis instructed to grasp multiple unseen objects sequentially\n(e.g., “grasp the bear then the bottle”). Specifically, the robot\nexecutes the grasp with the highest confidence, and inference\nis then repeated until all objects have been grasped. None of\nthe objects are seen during training or validation.\nWe repeated the grasp attempts 30 times for each case,\nand for two non-native English speakers. The total attempts\nwere 120. We present the results in Table V. The cascaded\npipeline requires a transcription step. However, because the\nreal-world speech commands are short and lack contextual\ncues, they are more prone to mistranscriptions than clean\nspeech. Table IV provides some examples. Once such errors\noccur, the grasp attempt will fail completely because the\ntarget object is not present, as illustrated in Figure 1a and\nthe corresponding video submission. Our Speech2Grasp, natively trained on speech, provides a better grasp detection rate\ndue to its tolerance to mild acoustic and speaker variations.\nNote that the improvement in the simulation experiments\nat Section V-A is modest because the speech prompts are\nclean and synthesized, allowing ASR to perform well. The\nlarger gains in the real-world experiments arise because\nthe ASR produces more mistranscriptions. Speech2Grasp’s\nrobustness can be attributed to the speech projector, whose\nresilience under much more challenging acoustic conditions\nwas demonstrated in the diagnostic study at Section III-C.\n\nPerson\n\nMethod\n\nSingle-object(↑)\n\nMulti-object(↑)\n\n#1\n\nLiteASR → LGD\nSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n\n0.59 ± 0.03\n0.70 ± 0.02\n\n0.54 ± 0.06\n0.61 ± 0.05\n\n#2\n\nLiteASR → LGD\nSpeech2Grasp (Ours)\n\n0.61 ± 0.04\n0.72 ± 0.03\n\n0.56 ± 0.07\n0.63 ± 0.06\n\nTABLE V\nR EAL - WORLD G RASP D ETECTION R ESULTS ON A H UMANOID ROBOT\n\nIn this paper, we first conduct diagnostic analyses to\nshow that a data-efficient, lightweight MLP-based projector effectively aligns speech embeddings into the ALBEF\nlatent space, while preserving semantic discrimination and\nrobustness to acoustic and speaker variations. Motivated by\nthese findings, we propose Speech2Grasp, a data-efficient\nframework that transfers text-conditioned grasp detection\nto speech. Simulation experiments show that Speech2Grasp\nachieves grasp performance comparable to the text-based\nteacher, while being data-efficient and faster in inference\ncompared to an ASR-based pipeline. Real-world experiments\non a humanoid robot demonstrate its deployability under\nreal-world acoustic conditions, while avoiding discrete mistranscription errors often occurring in real-world speech.\nDiscussion and Future Works. Firstly, it would be\nvaluable to investigate whether the proposed speech transfer\nparadigm generalizes to vision-language architectures with\nmulti-modal fusion strategies different from that of ALBEF.\nSecondly, the teacher-student framework incurs substantial GPU memory overhead due to the forward passes of\nboth teacher and student networks during training. Future\nwork will investigate cached knowledge distillation by precomputing teacher embeddings, enabling more memoryefficient speech transfer. Broadly speaking, we hope our findings suggest a practical paradigm for extending established\ntext-conditioned systems to speech, supporting the growing\ndemand for natural speech interaction in humanoid robots.\nR EFERENCES\n[1] J. A. Duncan, F. Alambeigi, and M. W. Pryor, “A survey of\nmultimodal perception methods for human–robot interaction in social\nenvironments,” J. Hum.-Robot Interact., vol. 13, no. 4, Oct. 2024.\n[Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.1145/3657030\n[2] X. Yao, H. Zhou, O. Mees, Y. Meng, T. Xiao, Y. Bisk, J. Oh,\nE. Johns, M. Shridhar, D. Shah, J. Thomason, K. Huang, J. Chai,\nZ. Bing, and A. Knoll, “Bridging language and action: A survey of\nlanguage-conditioned robot manipulation,” 2026. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10807\n\n\f[3] R. Girdhar, A. El-Nouby, Z. Liu, M. Singh, K. V. Alwala, A. Joulin,\nand I. Misra, “Imagebind: One embedding space to bind them all,” in\nCVPR, 2023.\n[4] B. Zhu, B. Lin, M. Ning, Y. Yan, J. Cui, W. HongFa, Y. Pang,\nW. Jiang, J. Zhang, Z. Li, C. W. Zhang, Z. Li, W. Liu, and L. Yuan,\n“Languagebind: Extending video-language pretraining to n-modality\nby language-based semantic alignment,” 2023.\n[5] J. Han, K. Gong, Y. Zhang, J. Wang, K. Zhang, D. Lin, Y. Qiao, P. Gao,\nand X. Yue, “Onellm: One framework to align all modalities with\nlanguage,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer\nVision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2024.\n[6] Y. Wu*, K. Chen*, T. Zhang*, Y. Hui*, T. Berg-Kirkpatrick, and\nS. Dubnov, “Large-scale contrastive language-audio pretraining with\nfeature fusion and keyword-to-caption augmentation,” in IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing,\nICASSP, 2023.\n[7] W. Tan, H. Inaguma, N. Dong, P. D. Tomasello, and X. Ma, “SSR:\nAlignment-aware modality connector for speech language models,”\nin Proceedings of the 22nd International Conference on Spoken\nLanguage Translation (IWSLT 2025), E. Salesky, M. Federico, and\nA. Anastasopoulos, Eds. Vienna, Austria (in-person and online):\nAssociation for Computational Linguistics, Jul. 2025, pp. 56–75.\n[Online]. Available: https://aclanthology.org/2025.iwslt-1.5/\n[8] R. Prabhavalkar, T. Hori, T. N. Sainath, R. Schlüter, and S. Watanabe,\n“End-to-end speech recognition: A survey,” IEEE/ACM Transactions\non Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 32, pp. 325–351,\n2024.\n[9] J. Li, R. R. Selvaraju, A. D. Gotmare, S. Joty, C. Xiong, and S. C.\nHoi, “Align before fuse: vision and language representation learning\nwith momentum distillation,” in Proceedings of the 35th International\nConference on Neural Information Processing Systems, ser. NIPS ’21.\nRed Hook, NY, USA: Curran Associates Inc., 2021.\n[10] A. D. Vuong, M. N. Vu, H. Le, B. Huang, H. T. T. Binh, T. Vo,\nA. Kugi, and A. Nguyen, “Grasp-anything: Large-scale grasp dataset\nfrom foundation models,” in 2024 IEEE International Conference on\nRobotics and Automation (ICRA), 2024, pp. 14 030–14 037.\n[11] J. Ni, Y. Ma, W. Wang, Q. Chen, D. Ng, H. Lei, T. H. Nguyen,\nC. Zhang, B. Ma, and E. Cambria, “Adaptive knowledge distillation\nbetween text and speech pre-trained models,” ICASSP 2023 2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and\nSignal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–5, 2023. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:257106768\n[12] Y. Xie, Y. Cui, L. Xie, and E. Yin, “Afd-slu: Adaptive feature\ndistillation for spoken language understanding,” in ICASSP 2026 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP), 2026, pp. 19 432–19 436.\n[13] H. Nie, Z. Zhao, L. Chen, Z. Lu, Z. Li, and J. Yang, “Smaller and\nfaster robotic grasp detection model via knowledge distillation and\nunequal feature encoding,” IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters,\nvol. 9, no. 8, pp. 7206–7213, 2024.\n[14] L. Peng, R. Cai, J. Xiang, J. Zhu, W. Liu, W. Gao, and Y. Liu, “Litegrasp: A light robotic grasp detection via semi-supervised knowledge\ndistillation,” IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters, vol. 9, no. 9, pp.\n7995–8002, 2024.\n[15] Y. Jiang, Y. Fang, and L. Deng, “Pdcnet: A lightweight\nand efficient robotic grasp detection framework via partial\nconvolution and knowledge distillation,” Computer Vision and Image\nUnderstanding, vol. 259, p. 104441, 2025. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S107731422500164X\n[16] A. D. Vuong, M. N. Vu, B. Huang, N. Nguyen, H. Le, T. Vo, and\nA. Nguyen, “Language-driven grasp detection,” in 2024 IEEE/CVF\nConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR),\n2024, pp. 17 902–17 912.\n[17] S. Noh, J. Kim, D. Nam, S. Back, R. Kang, and K. Lee, “Graspsam:\nWhen segment anything model meets grasp detection,” in 2025 IEEE\nInternational Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA), 2025,\npp. 14 023–14 029.\n[18] A. Kirillov, E. Mintun, N. Ravi, H. Mao, C. Rolland, L. Gustafson,\nT. Xiao, S. Whitehead, A. C. Berg, W.-Y. Lo, P. Dollár, and R. Girshick, “Segment anything,” arXiv:2304.02643, 2023.\n[19] T. Nguyen, M. N. Vu, B. Huang, A. Vuong, Q. Vuong, N. Le, T. Vo,\nand A. Nguyen, “Language-driven 6-dof grasp detection using negative\nprompt guidance,” in ECCV, 2024.\n[20] KittenML,\n“Kittentts:\nState-of-the-art\nlightweight\n\ntext-to-speech\nmodel,”\nGitHub\nrepository,\n2026,\nhttps://github.com/KittenML/KittenTTS.\n[21] A. Radford, J. W. Kim, T. Xu, G. Brockman, C. McLeavey, and\nI. Sutskever, “Robust speech recognition via large-scale weak supervision,” in International Conference on Machine Learning, 2022. [Online]. Available: https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:252923993\n[22] L. van der Maaten and G. Hinton, “Visualizing data\nusing t-SNE,” Journal of Machine Learning Research,\nvol.\n9,\npp.\n2579–2605,\n2008.\n[Online].\nAvailable:\nhttp://www.jmlr.org/papers/v9/vandermaaten08a.html\n[23] T. Ko, V. Peddinti, D. Povey, and S. Khudanpur, “A study on data\naugmentation of reverberant speech for robust speech recognition,”\nin IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP), 2017.\n[24] D. Jurafsky and J. H. Martin, Speech and Language Processing,\n2nd ed. Prentice Hall, 2009.\n[25] J. Devlin, M.-W. Chang, K. Lee, and K. Toutanova,\n“Bert: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for\nlanguage understanding,” in North American Chapter of the\nAssociation for Computational Linguistics, 2019. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:52967399\n[26] H. Nguyen, B. R. Li, and T. Nguyen, “Dwtnerf: Boosting few-shot\nneural radiance fields via discrete wavelet transform,” 2025. [Online].\nAvailable: https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12637\n[27] H. Nguyen, R. Li, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Dwtgs: Rethinking\nfrequency regularization for sparse-view 3d gaussian splatting,” in\nProceedings of the 2025 IEEE International Conference on Visual\nCommunications and Image Processing (VCIP). IEEE, 2025.\n[28] H. Nguyen, A. Le, B. R. Li, and T. Nguyen, “From coarse to\nfine: Learnable discrete wavelet transforms for efficient 3d gaussian\nsplatting,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference\non Computer Vision (ICCV) Workshops, October 2025, pp. 3139–3148.\n[29] H. Nguyen, R. Li, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Waveletgaussian: Waveletdomain diffusion for sparse-view 3d gaussian object reconstruction,” in\nProceedings of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2026.\n[30] H. Nguyen, A. Le, and T. Nguyen, “Learnable multi-level discrete\nwavelet transforms for 3d gaussian splatting frequency modulation,”\n2026. [Online]. Available: https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14199\n[31] H. Phung, Q. Dao, and A. Tran, “Wavelet diffusion models are fast\nand scalable image generators,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\nConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), June\n2023, pp. 10 199–10 208.\n[32] A. Gupta, P. Dollár, and R. Girshick, “Lvis: A dataset for large\nvocabulary instance segmentation,” in 2019 IEEE/CVF Conference on\nComputer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2019, pp. 5351–\n5359.\n[33] K. Zhou, J. Yang, C. C. Loy, and Z. Liu, “Conditional prompt\nlearning for vision-language models,” in 2022 IEEE/CVF Conference\non Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2022, pp.\n16 795–16 804.\n[34] K. Kamahori, J. Kasai, N. Kojima, and B. Kasikci, “Liteasr: Efficient\nautomatic speech recognition with low-rank approximation,” in Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural\nLanguage Processing (EMNLP).\nAssociation for Computational\nLinguistics, 2025, pp. 2047–2061.\n[35] N. Bähler, M. El Helou, É. Objois, K. Okumuş, and S. Süsstrunk,\n“Pogain: Poisson-gaussian image noise modeling from paired samples,” IEEE Signal Processing Letters, vol. 29, pp. 2602–2606, 2022.\n[36] Unitree\nRobotics,\n“Unitree\nrobotics\nofficial\nwebsite,”\nhttps://www.unitree.com/, n.d., accessed: 2026-03-03.\n\n\f", "numLines": 845, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 845}}}
